Notification retry authority and attempt lifecycle
=================================================

The retry worker reads the persisted delivery before any provider call. Its
organization, channel, subject, failed state and prior attempt must match the
task. Successful, skipped, dead-lettered, missing, foreign and stale attempts
are refused. A tenant must still be active and not deleted. Invalid identifiers
or attempts are refused without delivery. The total attempt ceiling is five,
including the initial send; an explicitly lower ceiling remains supported.

A PostgreSQL row lock serializes workers retrying the same delivery. A worker
that waits for another worker's committed result rechecks the eligibility
predicate before sending. This lock is held across the provider call and commit;
worker time limits still apply. Tests using SQLite cannot establish this lock
behavior and must be supplemented by real PostgreSQL qualification.

Explicit provider IDs must identify an enabled provider in the exact delivery
organization and channel. A missing or ineligible explicit provider does not
fall back to a different provider. Default tenant retries load only that tenant's
enabled providers. A platform-scoped retry can select only a platform provider.
These are lookup-time lifecycle checks; concurrent provider/tenant changes are
not atomically fenced with the external delivery.

The scheduling helper returns whether the broker call completed successfully.
It emits retry_scheduled only after that call. Broker failures emit a fixed
failure log without exception text, and the worker reports rescheduled=false.
Logs are not a durable retry payload or dead-letter queue. A broker timeout can
be ambiguous, so a failed call is not proof that no task reached the broker.

Provider exceptions become a fixed failure message. Provider-reported errors
retain their existing classification/storage behavior; this correction does not
claim all provider responses are scrubbed. Successful provider skips are stored
as skipped, not sent. Failed or uncertain commits are never reported as success.

Remaining limits
----------------
The original dispatcher may still enqueue before its outer transaction commits
and may use one AsyncSession concurrently. This worker correction does not make
those initial transactions durable. Retry content and recipient still come from
the trusted task payload, not a persisted immutable outbox. Protect broker write
access accordingly. A provider effect followed by a crash or failed commit can
still be repeated by a later retry; exactly-once delivery is not guaranteed.
Retry provider calls retain their existing rate-limit/preference behavior; this
change does not establish a new end-to-end delivery or spending guarantee.

Synthetic provider mocks and short database fixtures are not external SMTP,
SMS, webhook or live hardware acceptance. Do not describe these guards alone as
enterprise production readiness.
